    "summary": "Epic Lovelight suite, soaring Easy Wind, strong acoustic set",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a marathon Turn On Your Lovelight suite and fiery Good Lovin' jams. Jerry’s tone soars on the acoustic numbers while the band locks into high‑energy electric stretches. A solid pick for fans of early‑70s Dead power.",
    "review": "On May 7, 1970 at MIT’s Dupont Gymnasium the Grateful Dead delivered a historic three‑set marathon that still thrills listeners. The first set unfolded as an unusually long acoustic showcase, highlighting tight renditions of “Candyman,” “Cumberland Blues,” and “Uncle John’s Band.” The second set erupted into electric fireworks – a ripping “Good Lovin'” jam, a soaring “Easy Wind,” and the centerpiece “Turn On Your Lovelight” suite that stretches over ten minutes with Jerry’s guitar tone on full display. The deep‑sea dive of “The Other One” paired with the cryptic “Cryptical Envelopment” moments showcases the band’s improvisational daring, while Mickey’s drumming punctuates “Beat It On Down The Line” with playful call‑outs. Though the audience tapes carry period hiss and occasional mic chatter, SirMick’s restoration work makes this recording one of the clearer sources for an early‑70s show, capturing the raw excitement that made the MIT gig a legendary entry in Dead history.",
